INT. DIALYSIS UNIT - NIGHT

Chairs in a row. Machines whirring. One patient. One nurse.

ABIOLA SOYINKA, 40s, nurse, ID badge clipped to scrubs, checks a monitor. MR. CASTELLANOS, 70s, patient, in the third chair, arm in the machine, stares at the ceiling.

ABIOLA
Pressure's good.

CASTELLANOS
Always is.

ABIOLA
Tonight?

CASTELLANOS
Always.

She adjusts the line. He doesn't flinch.

Quiet. The machine hums.

CASTELLANOS (CONT'D)
My daughter called.

ABIOLA
That's nice.

CASTELLANOS
She's coming Thursday.

ABIOLA
That's nice.

He turns his head. Studies her.

CASTELLANOS
You say that twice.

ABIOLA
Do I?

CASTELLANOS
You do when you don't believe it.

She smiles. A small one. Checks the clock.

An alarm shrieks. Both flinch. Abiola spins to the monitor. Red.

ABIOLA
Mr. Castellanos. Look at me.

CASTELLANOS
I'm fine.

ABIOLA
Stay still.

She reaches for his arm. He pulls it away. The line tugs. Blood beads at the needle.

CASTELLANOS
Don't.

ABIOLA
You'll hurt yourself.

CASTELLANOS
I want to go home.

ABIOLA
Not yet.

He rips at the tape. She seizes his wrist. For a moment they grapple, nurse and patient, old hands and young. The machine screams. The chair rocks.

Then he stops. Goes limp. She holds his wrist. He holds her gaze.

CASTELLANOS (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Thursday.

ABIOLA
Thursday.

She resets the machine. The alarm falls silent. She presses gauze to his arm. He lets her.

CASTELLANOS
You say it right that time.

ABIOLA
I know.

She tapes the gauze down. Checks the line. Pats his hand. Neither speaks. The machine settles back into its slow, patient hum.
